This one requires log analysis to see what happened in a breach of a juice shop.

https://tryhackme.com/room/juicydetails

KEY TAKEAWAYS
I learned how to investigate an actual incident. I'm sure I would have to dig a bit more in a live investigation, and that's exactly why I'm practicing now. It was a great experience to use my analysis muscles and see if I can figure out what happened and when.

What tools did the attacker use? (Order by the occurrence in the log)
I did an initial read of the log file provided rather than jumping into scripting for analysis. Early logs show Nmap (a network scanning tool, commonly used for discovery and reconnaissance) in the second line:
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:08:34 +0000] "POST / HTTP/1.1" 200 1924 "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Nmap Scripting Engine; https://nmap.org/book/nse.html)"

Then, Hydra (a password cracker) is indicated:
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:16:27 +0000] "GET /rest/user/login HTTP/1.0" 500 - "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (Hydra)"

SQLMap (used to launch SQL injection attaks) is also logged:
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:29:14 +0000] "GET /rest/products/search?q=1 HTTP/1.1" 200 - "-" "sqlmap/1.5.2#stable (http://sqlmap.org)"

Curl was also used (commonly leveraged for data transfer):
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:32:51 +0000] "GET /rest/products/search?q=qwert%27))%20UNION%20SELECT%20id,%20email,%20password,%20%274%27,%20%275%27,%20%276%27,%20%277%27,%20%278%27,%20%279%27%20FROM%20Users-- HTTP/1.1" 200 3742 "-" "curl/7.74.0"

And finally, feroxbuster (used in forced browsing attacks) is identified:
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:34:33 +0000] "GET /a54372a1404141fe8842ae5c029a00e3 HTTP/1.1" 200 1924 "-" "feroxbuster/2.2.1"

2. What endpoint was vulnerable to a brute-force attack?
If we were paying careful attention to the logs in this file earlier, we would see a chunk of logs that look something like this:

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:16:27 +0000] "GET /rest/user/login HTTP/1.0" 500 - "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (Hydra)"
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:16:27 +0000] "GET /rest/user/login HTTP/1.0" 500 - "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (Hydra)"
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:16:27 +0000] "GET /rest/user/login HTTP/1.0" 500 - "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (Hydra)"
[...]

There are a few red flags here:
Several logs were recorded with this exact line
The URL accessed is a user login page
The attempts are all within the time frame of one second
Hydra, a known password cracking tool, is being used

It appears that a brute force password attack is being attempted.

3. What endpoint was vulnerable to SQL injection?
This time, I was searching for anything that looks like it might be SQL.

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:29:15 +0000] "GET /rest/products/search?q=%28SELECT%20%28CASE%20WHEN%20%288195%3D8761%29%20THEN%201%20ELSE%20%28SELECT%208761%20UNION%20SELECT%203788%29%20END%29%29 HTTP/1.1" 200 30 "-" "sqlmap/1.5.2#stable (http://sqlmap.org)"

(There was a bit of SQL in the previous few logs, but this one has a full SELECT statement.)

4. What parameter was used for the SQL injection?
Take one of these lines as an example:

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:29:15 +0000] "GET /rest/products/search?q=1%27cjocta%3C%27%22%3ETuFsMe HTTP/1.1" 500 - "-" "sqlmap/1.5.2#stable (http://sqlmap.org)"

The "search?q=" is indicative of using the search box labeled with a q to run a query. Thus, q is the parameter being exploited.

5. What endpoint did the attacker try to use to retrieve files? (Include the /)
Towards the end of the log file, there are some lines that reveal FTP being utilized.

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:34:40 +0000] "GET /ftp/www-data.bak HTTP/1.1" 403 300 "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:78.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/78.0"
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:34:43 +0000] "GET /ftp/coupons_2013.md.bak HTTP/1.1" 403 78965 "-" ""Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:78.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/78.0"

This suggests that the attacker is trying to GET (or download) the files at ftp/www-data.bak and ftp/coupons_2013.md.bak. Therefore, the endpoint being attacked is the /ftp endpoint, likely a file hosting server.


6. What section of the website did the attacker use to scrape user email addresses?
Analysis shows the user ran "whoami" and then references a review page:

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:09:23 +0000] "GET /rest/user/whoami HTTP/1.1" 200 11 "http://192.168.10.4/" "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:78.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/78.0"
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:09:23 +0000] "GET /rest/products/1/reviews HTTP/1.1" 200 172 "http://192.168.10.4/" "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:78.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/78.0"
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:09:23 +0000] "GET /rest/products/1/reviews HTTP/1.1" 200 172 "http://192.168.10.4/" "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:78.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/78.0"
::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:09:24 +0000] "GET /rest/products/1/reviews HTTP/1.1" 304 - "http://192.168.10.4/" "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:78.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/78.0"

They're using the product reviews area to gather information.

7. Was their brute-force attack successful? If so, what is the timestamp of the successful login? (Yay/Nay, 11/Apr/2021:09:xx:xx +0000)
There's one log where the HTTP code given is 200, which means "OK".

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:16:31 +0000] "POST /rest/user/login HTTP/1.0" 200 831 "-" "Mozilla/5.0 (Hydra)"

Yay, 11/Apr/2021:09:16:31 +0000.


8. What user information was the attacker able to retrieve from the endpoint vulnerable to SQL injection?
Direct attention towards last SQL statement inserted:

::ffff:192.168.10.5 - - [11/Apr/2021:09:32:51 +0000] "GET /rest/products/search?q=qwert%27))%20UNION%20SELECT%20id,%20email,%20password,%20%274%27,%20%275%27,%20%276%27,%20%277%27,%20%278%27,%20%279%27%20FROM%20Users-- HTTP/1.1" 200 3742 "-" "curl/7.74.0"

Deciphering the URL coding reveals it to say "UNION SELECT id, email, password." Id isn't relevant here, as it doesn't give any information to exploit. Therefore, the answer is just email and password.

9. What files did they try to download from the vulnerable endpoint? (endpoint from the previous task, question #5)
Refer back to my answer for #5. I already showed exactly what files were downloaded there.

10. What service and account name were used to retrieve files from the previous question? (service, username)
Pivoting to the other log, vsftpd.log, reveals:

Sun Apr 11 09:35:37 2021 [pid 8152] [ftp] OK LOGIN: Client "::ffff:192.168.10.5", anon password "?"
Sun Apr 11 09:35:45 2021 [pid 8154] [ftp] OK DOWNLOAD: Client "::ffff:192.168.10.5", "/www-data.bak", 2602 bytes, 544.81Kbyte/sec
Sun Apr 11 09:36:08 2021 [pid 8154] [ftp] OK DOWNLOAD: Client "::ffff:192.168.10.5", "/coupons_2013.md.bak", 131 bytes, 3.01Kbyte/sec

The last two lines are showing the FTP (service) transfer of the files we found earlier. Look at the first line, and it shows that an anonymous account was logged into to perform this action.

11. What service and username were used to gain shell access to the server? (service, username)
In auth.log, we can see one line showing an accepted attempt:

Apr 11 09:41:19 thunt sshd[8260]: Accepted password for www-data from 192.168.10.5 port 40112 ssh2

Looking at the end of the line, we see it was attempted via SSH. The username used is the one the password was accepted for, which appears to be www-data.
